D (starts with 'trig_'). Returned by create_trigger in the response's trigger.id field, or by list_triggers.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["trigger_id"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__fire_trigger"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Describe the cloud environment THIS session runs in, or, given `environment_id`, another environment the user can see (their own or one shared with their organization; ids come from list_environments). An environment is the saved setup a cloud session runs in: allowed network hosts, environment variables and a setup script. Returns its name and, for this session's own environment, its description; whether it is personal or shared with the organization; its network access (unrestricted, or the allowed hosts and whether package managers and MCP servers are allowed); the NAMES of its environment variables (values are never returned); its network secrets, if the user may edit it (host, header, name; never a value); whether it has a setup script (the script itself only when you could edit it); whether it is the environment this session runs in; and whether you could edit it with update_environment (`editable_by_you`; only this session's own environment can be). The description and script text are withheld for organizations with restricted compliance settings. This tool needs no approval, and you do not need to ask the user before calling it. Omit `environment_id` to describe this session's environment. When to propose a change to what a session can reach: you never widen it yourself. update_environment and create_environment each show the user an approval prompt, every time, in every permission mode, and nothing changes unless they approve. (1) If the current environment works for the task, do nothing: propose no change and show no approval prompt. Most sessions are like this. The one exception is saving a setup script, which update_environment and create_environment describe. (2) Propose only on a